Start here if you have an application repository and are setting up CI/CD for the first time. Follow this section to connect the repository, run automated checks, build the app, and verify delivery to users. React Native developers follow both Android and iOS steps when releasing to both platforms.

Begin with a working local application. If you still need development tools or help launching the app, complete [Machine Setup](../onboarding/machine-setup-guide/index.mdx) and [First Local Run](../onboarding/first-local-run.mdx).

## 1. What CI and CD do

**Continuous integration (CI)** runs checks when code changes. For example, a developer opens a pull request, automation runs the configured checks, and reviewers inspect the results before merging.

**Continuous delivery (CD)** prepares an approved build for its destination. This includes the required security assessment, signing, upload, and availability checks. The selected workflow and release approvals control when delivery can proceed.

| Term | Simple meaning |
|---|---|
| Repository | The Git project containing your files and their change history. |
| Commit | A recorded version of the files. Its commit ID identifies the code being built. |
| Pull request, or PR | A proposed change submitted for review. |
| Workflow | A named job containing ordered steps. |
| Pipeline | The configuration that connects build jobs and their dependencies. |
| Artifact | A file produced by a build, such as an Android APK/AAB or an iOS IPA. |
| Enrollment | The first-time process that connects an application to the governed build platform. |

