## 2. Open the application folder

Open the project in your editor, then open its terminal. The terminal must point to the application folder before you run the commands below.

For an existing remote application that you have not downloaded, follow [First Local Run](../onboarding/first-local-run.mdx) to clone the assigned repository and install its locked dependencies. Then return here and confirm the files for your platform.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React Native">

The root is the folder containing `package.json`. The supplied workshop checks for these native files too.

```text
application-root/
├── package.json
├── <project's package-manager lockfile>
├── android/
│   ├── gradlew
│   └── app/
│       └── build.gradle
└── ios/
    └── Podfile
```

A project may use a supported alternative Gradle build-file format. Follow its approved template's layout.

React Native needs both native toolchains when building Android and iOS. Record the Android application module and the iOS workspace/project and shared scheme from the existing project instructions.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Android">

Open the native Android project root containing the Gradle wrapper and application module.

```text
application-root/
├── gradlew
├── gradle/
│   └── wrapper/
├── settings.gradle or settings.gradle.kts
└── <application-module>/
    ├── build.gradle or build.gradle.kts
    └── src/
```

Use the module and variant documented by the project. Record its application ID and the build/test tasks that already work locally.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

Open the workspace or project specified by the application's setup guide.

```text
application-root/
├── <application>.xcodeproj/
├── <application>.xcworkspace/    # When the project uses a workspace
├── <application-source>/
└── <test-source>/               # When test targets are configured
```

Retain the project's shared schemes and applicable dependency locks. Record the workspace/project, scheme, and bundle identifiers used by the approved build.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 3. Identify the Git repository

Run the following in the application terminal. These commands inspect the current checkout.

```bash
git status --short --branch
git branch --show-current
git rev-parse HEAD
git remote get-url origin
```

| Command | What to read |
|---|---|
| `git status --short --branch` | Current branch and local changes. |
| `git branch --show-current` | Application branch name. A blank result requires checking the checkout state. |
| `git rev-parse HEAD` | Full commit ID of the current checkout. |
| `git remote get-url origin` | Address of the connected source repository. |

Confirm the repository is private in GitHub Enterprise and that you can access it. Use a repository URL without embedded passwords or tokens when recording or sharing details.

**Expected result —** you have identified the application checkout and its remote. If Git reports that this is not a repository, open the generated application's correct folder. If `origin` is missing, follow the approved private-repository publishing instructions.

## 4. Run the existing local checks

First install dependencies using the application's README and committed dependency locks. Then launch the app using its documented development configuration. For React Native, verify the native platforms you plan to release.

The supplied Bitrise workflow runs `npm test` for its npm application. The earlier preparation reference also supplies `npm run lint`. Run these commands only when the application defines the corresponding scripts.

```bash
npm test
npm run lint
```

Type `npm test` in the application terminal and wait for it to finish. Read the test result. If it passes, run `npm run lint` and read that result. Resolve a failure before continuing.

Each `npm` command looks up its script in `package.json`. These commands run on your Mac during preparation. Later, the Bitrise npm step runs the configured command on a build machine. The configuration repository has its own validation procedure.

| Platform | Local verification |
|---|---|
| React Native | Run the existing JavaScript/TypeScript checks and the documented Android/iOS builds. |
| Android | Run the project's configured Gradle tests and development build. |
| iOS | Run the project's configured Xcode tests and development build using its shared scheme. |

Use the approved starter's actual Gradle tasks, Xcode scheme, and test setup. [First Local Run](../onboarding/first-local-run.mdx) explains how to identify and launch the development target. Follow [Testing & Quality](../testing-quality/index.mdx) for writing tests, coverage configuration, and device-test preparation.

**Expected result —** the app launches in the required development environment, its configured tests execute, and local checks pass. Record the first failing command if you need help.

## 5. Publish and confirm the intended source

Review and publish source changes through your team's application PR process. After the accepted changes reach the application branch intended for enrollment, confirm that branch and commit in GitHub Enterprise.

The source branch must be compatible with the selected Bitrise template. The latest request example uses `development`. Confirm the branch allowed by the template you select before using that value. The enrollment page explains the catalog check.

Record these details for the request.

```text
Application name
Private application repository URL
Application source branch
Accepted source commit ID
Android application module and build variant, when applicable
iOS workspace/project and shared scheme, when applicable
Local commands executed and their results
```

Use the accepted remote branch's commit when local HEAD still identifies a separate feature branch or a revision changed by merging.

**Attach screenshot — application repository, intended branch, and accepted commit with sensitive details hidden.**

## If a step fails

| Problem | Next action |
|---|---|
| Expected application files are missing | Check that you opened the generated application rather than the starter repository. |
| Dependencies cannot be downloaded | Check the approved registry setup and your access using the project's setup instructions. |
| `npm test` or `npm run lint` is missing | Inspect `package.json` and ask the application owner for the approved checks. |
| App build or tests fail | Fix the first failure locally and repeat the affected check. |
| Source branch differs from the catalog requirement | Confirm the intended branch with the platform owner before writing the request. |
| Reviewed changes exist only on your Mac | Publish them through the application review process and confirm their remote commit. |

**Next page — [CAMP File Reference](./campfile-reference.mdx).** Add application metadata before continuing to the configuration repository and enrollment request.



