## 2. Read the supplied file schema

The following block preserves the field names, indentation, and comments from the latest reference. Text after `#` is a YAML comment. The empty entries still need actual values.

```yaml
app_name: #<app_name>
app_identifier: # set only one if not use multiple flavors/schemas
  dev: #<bundle_identifier/app_identifier/reverser_domain>
  stage: #<bundle_identifier/app_identifier/reverser_domain>
  prod: #<bundle_identifier/app_identifier/reverser_domain>
bitrise_app_name: #<bitrise_app_name> (this will be provided by the camp team)
bitrise_app_url: #<bitrise_app_url> (this will be provided by the camp team)
business_enterprise: #<entrepise_unit>
business_unit: #<business_unit>
datadog_dashboard: #<datadog_dashboard_url> (this will be provided by the camp team)
platform_type: #<code_platform>
region: #<region>
repository_name: #<repo_name>
repository_url: #<repo_url>
apperian_name: #[optional]
  dev: #<app_name_store>
  uat: #<app_name_store>
  prod: #<app_name_store>
```

### Confirm one format difference before copying

The schema above uses mappings for `app_identifier` and `apperian_name`. The supplied example below uses lists of mappings for those fields. These produce different data structures. Ask the CAMP consumer owner which form it accepts before publishing. Keep an existing accepted file in its supported format while that question is resolved.

## 3. Fill in the application values

| Field | What to enter | Who supplies it |
|---|---|---|
| `app_name` | Approved application name. | Application owner. |
| `app_identifier` | Actual identifiers for the supported environments or flavors. | Application build configuration and owner. |
| `bitrise_app_name` | Provisioned Bitrise application name. | CAMP team. |
| `bitrise_app_url` | Provisioned Bitrise application URL. | CAMP team. |
| `business_enterprise` | Accepted enterprise classification. | Application owner. |
| `business_unit` | Accepted business unit within that classification. | Application owner. |
| `datadog_dashboard` | Approved application dashboard URL. | CAMP team. |
| `platform_type` | `ios`, `android`, `react_native`, or `pwa`. | Application platform. |
| `region` | Accepted region with the documented casing. | Application owner. |
| `repository_name` | Application repository name. | GitHub Enterprise repository. |
| `repository_url` | Application repository URL without embedded credentials. | GitHub Enterprise repository. |
| `apperian_name` | Optional destination application names. | Distribution owner. |

**For a new app**, the provisioned Bitrise URL may be unavailable before enrollment. The reference does not define whether initial validation accepts an omitted value, an empty value, or a later metadata update. Obtain that setup rule from the CAMP team before submitting the file.

### Supplied example

This is the reference example. Its three private service/repository URLs use marked placeholders. All other YAML content is preserved. Replace application-specific values with your approved configuration after confirming the field shapes.

```yaml
app_name: cocoapp
app_identifier:
  - dev: com.enterprise.cocoapp
  - stage: com.enterprise.cocoapp
  - prod: com.enterprise.cocoapp
bitrise_app_name: cocoapp
bitrise_app_url: "REPLACE_WITH_PROVISIONED_BITRISE_APP_URL"
business_enterprise: trade
business_unit: trading_and_risk
datadog_dashboard: "REPLACE_WITH_APPROVED_DATADOG_DASHBOARD_URL"
platform_type: react_native
region: EMEA
repository_name: coco_app_frontend
repository_url: "REPLACE_WITH_APPROVED_APPLICATION_REPOSITORY_URL"
apperian_name:
  - dev: "cocoapp-dev"
  - uat: "cocoapp-uat"
  - prod: "cocoapp"
```

React Native uses **`react_native`** in CAMP. The enrollment request uses **`react-native`** in its separate `platform` field. Preserve each field’s accepted spelling.

