## 5. Understand analytics integration

The reference identifies these four tracked iOS attributes.

```text
app_name
business_enterprise
business_unit
platform_type
```

Its analytics instruction limits iOS tracking to this list. It names **CampfileLib** for iOS and marks the Android and React Native libraries **TBD**. Obtain the approved iOS library version and integration instructions from its owner. Android and React Native reader APIs remain unspecified in the supplied material.

The browser RUM script shown in the reference instruments the developer portal. For mobile SDK setup, use [Datadog Mobile Integration](../observability/datadog-mobile-integration.mdx). Confirm which application component reads CAMP and how the approved attributes reach analytics.

## 6. Validate and publish

1. Confirm the accepted mapping/list format with the CAMP consumer owner.
2. Fill in the real application values and obtain the platform-service values.
3. Run the CAMP validation procedure provided by that owner.
4. Review the file through the application repository’s PR process.
5. Confirm the accepted file is present on the application source branch intended for enrollment.

The supplied reference contains no standalone CAMP validator command or complete machine-readable validation schema. Obtain the actual procedure before declaring this step complete. YAML parsing alone checks document structure, while the consumer determines accepted field shapes and values.

**Expected result —** the application root contains a reviewed CAMP file in the format accepted by its tooling, with any enrollment-time metadata dependency resolved.

