**Bitrise runs the commands that check, build, and deliver your application.** Its YAML configuration names the jobs and defines the steps they execute.

**Work location — the enrolled application in Bitrise and its approved workflow configuration.** Follow [Review & Promote](./review-and-promote.mdx) first and identify your existing Bitrise app and apply result.

## 1. Find the configuration used by your app

1. Open the Bitrise application identified in the platform apply result.
2. Confirm its application repository and source branch.
3. Identify the approved template ID, version, and catalog revision.
4. Ask the platform owner where Bitrise loads the workflow YAML and where application-specific changes are permitted.
5. Inspect the selected workflow and runner configuration.

The source of the build configuration must be confirmed before editing `bitrise.yml`. The approved catalog owns versioned workflow definitions. Follow its review process for shared template changes.

**Attach screenshot — enrolled application, workflow configuration source, and selected stack with sensitive values hidden.**

## 2. Understand the execution levels

| YAML level | Simple meaning | Example from the reference |
|---|---|---|
| Pipeline | Connects the stages of a build process. | `release-pipeline` |
| Stage | Groups workflows at one phase. | `test-stage` |
| Workflow | A named job containing steps. | `unit-tests` |
| Step | One operation inside a workflow. | The npm step that runs tests. |
| Script | Commands executed by a shell inside a step. | The supplied script that installs and checks `saucectl`. |

<MermaidZoom title="Read the supplied pipeline from top to bottom">

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Prepare stage<br/>setup"] --> B["Test stage<br/>unit-tests + lint-and-security"]
    B --> C["Build stage<br/>build-android + build-ios"]
    C --> D["Distribution stage<br/>deploy-internal"]
    classDef step fill:#172554,stroke:#93c5fd,color:#ffffff,stroke-width:2px;
