```

</MermaidZoom>

Stages describe sequential phases. Workflows grouped in one stage can run concurrently when the configured dependencies and available runners permit it. Step results and the workflow's failure settings determine whether execution proceeds.

## 3. Read the supplied pipeline YAML

This block preserves the latest reference exactly. It describes the pipeline and stages. The referenced workflow bodies are omitted in the supplied block, so the approved complete configuration must supply `setup`, `unit-tests`, `lint-and-security`, `build-android`, `build-ios`, and `deploy-internal`.

```yaml
format_version: '11'
default_step_lib_source: https://github.com/bitrise-io/bitrise-steplib.git
project_type: react-native

pipelines:
  release-pipeline:
    stages:
      - prepare-stage: {}
      - test-stage: {}
      - build-stage: {}
      - distribution-stage: {}

stages:
  prepare-stage:
    workflows:
      - setup: {}
  test-stage:
    workflows:
      - unit-tests: {}
      - lint-and-security: {}
  build-stage:
    workflows:
      - build-android: {}
      - build-ios: {}
  distribution-stage:
    workflows:
      - deploy-internal: {}
```

| Entry | What to understand |
|---|---|
| `format_version: '11'` | Configuration-format value used by this reference. |
| `default_step_lib_source` | Default library used to resolve standard step identifiers. |
| `project_type: react-native` | Project type declared by the example. |
| `pipelines` | Pipeline names and their stage order. |
| `stages` | Workflows grouped into each stage. |

The example stage named `lint-and-security` does not define its checks. The workflow body must show what it actually executes. A binary assessment needs the appropriate built package available at its configured execution point.

## 4. Understand the four governed routes

The platform enrollment defines these route identities. Inspect the selected template for their actual workflow names, build targets, and destinations.

| Route | Platform and purpose |
|---|---|
| `qa_android` | Android QA build and its configured checks/distribution. |
| `qa_ios` | iOS QA build and its configured checks/distribution. |
| `uat_android` | Android UAT build and its configured checks/distribution. |
| `uat_ios` | iOS UAT build and its configured checks/distribution. |

The pipeline example above uses generic workflow names. Confirm how your approved template implements the four routes. Record each route's source commit, build ID, artifacts, and final result separately.

## 5. Read the supplied Android and Sauce Labs workflow

The following `deploy` workflow is reproduced exactly from the latest reference. It is an Android/React Native integration example. Read its prerequisites below before using it in the approved configuration.

```yaml
workflows:
  deploy:
    steps:
      # 1. Clone the repository
      - git-clone@8: {}

      # 2. Install dependencies
      - npm@3:
          title: npm install
          inputs:
            - workdir: "$WORKDIR"
            - command: install

      # 3. Run unit tests
      - npm@3:
          title: npm test
          inputs:
            - workdir: "$WORKDIR"
            - command: test

      # 4. Set up Android build tools
      - install-missing-android-tools@3: {}

      # 5. Build the Android app
      - android-build@1:
          inputs:
            - project_location: "$PROJECT_LOCATION"
            - module: "$MODULE"
            - variant: "$VARIANT"

      # 6. [Optional] Install saucectl for running tests
      - script@1:
          inputs:
            - content: |-
                npm install -g saucectl
