## 3. Create the rule file

**File — `src/danger/dangerfile.ts`.** This block preserves the supplied rule behavior. Two broken multiline string literals in the transcription are repaired to use `\n`. The rule logic and messages are retained.

```typescript
import { message, danger, fail, warn } from "danger"

const modifiedMD = danger.git.modified_files.join("\n")
message("Changed Files in this PR: \n" + modifiedMD)

if (!danger.github.pr.assignee) {
  const method = danger.github.pr.title.includes("WIP") ? warn : fail;
  method("This pull request needs an assignee, and optionally include any reviewers.");
}

if (!danger.github.pr.body || danger.github.pr.body.length < 10) {
  fail("This request needs a description");
}
```

| PR condition | Supplied behavior |
|---|---|
| Modified files | Posts the names in `danger.git.modified_files`. |
| Missing assignee and title contains `WIP` | Adds a warning. The title match is case-sensitive. |
| Missing assignee and title does not contain `WIP` | Adds a failure. |
| Missing description or fewer than 10 characters | Adds a failure. |

A description with exactly 10 characters passes this rule. Reviewer enforcement, draft blocking, conventional-title validation, size limits, and changelog checks require additional approved rules or repository policy. The latest code above implements the behaviors in this table.

